<section xml:id="sec-mod1-assessment">
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <p>Estimated time: 40 minutes.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

        <p>
          This assessment has three parts. Parts 1 and 2 ask you to write
          complete mathematical proofs, submitted for feedback. Part 3 is
          a short reflection. Submit all three parts together as a single
          document (PDF or typed file) through the course management system.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Write-up standard.</alert> All proofs must be written in
          complete mathematical prose. Use the style modeled in the Worked
          Examples: label your proof strategy in the opening sentence, write
          in complete grammatical sentences, and justify every step. Work
          that consists only of symbolic manipulations without explanation
          will not receive full credit.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>A note on Part 2.</alert> All students complete all three
          parts. Part 2 offers two options. Option A asks for a proof by
          induction of a divisibility statement. Option B asks for a proof
          by contrapositive (with cases) of a divisibility lemma, then a
          proof by contradiction that <m>\sqrt{6}</m> is irrational, and is the more
          demanding. Choose the option that will stretch you.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Academic integrity.</alert> Your proofs must be your own
          work. You may refer to your notes, the textbooks, and the worked
          examples from this module. You may not collaborate with other
          students on the written proofs or use AI writing tools to draft
          your arguments.
        </p>
      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-as-part1">
        <title>Part 1: Core Proof (Required)</title>
        <p>
          Estimated time: 20 minutes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Recall the definition of sequence convergence:
          <md>
            \lim_{n \to \infty} a_n = L \;\;\text{means}\;\;
            \forall \varepsilon \gt 0 \;\; \exists N \in \N
            \text{ s.t. } \forall n \in \N,\;\; n \geq N \Rightarrow |a_n - L| \lt \varepsilon.
          </md>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-as-1a">
        <title>Negating the Convergence Definition</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Write, in careful symbolic notation, the statement that
            <m>a_n \not\to L</m> (the sequence does <em>not</em>
            converge to <m>L</m>). Your answer should be the
            formal negation of the definition above, with all
            quantifiers written explicitly and in the correct order.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-as-1b">
        <title>Proving Convergence: <m>a_n = (2n+1)/n</m></title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>a_n = \dfrac{2n + 1}{n}</m>. Prove that
            <m>\lim_{n \to \infty} a_n = 2</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Your proof must follow this structure:
            <ol marker="(i)">
              <li>
                <em>Scratch work (clearly labeled, before the proof).</em>
                Compute <m>|a_n - 2|</m> and find, given
                <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, an explicit <m>N</m>
                (in terms of <m>\varepsilon</m>) such that
                <m>n \geq N</m> guarantees <m>|a_n - 2| \lt \varepsilon</m>.
                You may use any algebraic manipulation here; this
                is your planning stage.
              </li>
              <li>
                <em>Formal proof.</em> Using the <m>N</m> you found
                in the scratch work, write a complete
                <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> proof in standard
                mathematical prose. The proof should begin:
                <q>Let <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> be given.
                Choose <m>N = \ldots</m></q>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
          <p>
            In the scratch work, write <m>a_n - 2</m> as a single fraction
            before taking the absolute value, then ask how large <m>n</m>
            must be. In the formal proof, say which form of the Archimedean
            Property (see
            <xref ref="par-mod1-sg-archimedean" text="custom">the three forms in the Study Guide</xref>,
            or Bauldry's Corollary 2.4, p. 48) guarantees that the
            <m>N</m> you choose exists in <m>\N</m>.
          </p>
        </hint>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-as-1c">
        <title>Applying the Negation</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Using your negation from <xref ref="ex-mod1-as-1a"/>, write out what it
            would mean for the sequence <m>a_n = (2n+1)/n</m> to <em>not</em>
            converge to 3. (You do not need to prove this statement;
            just write it in symbols and translate it into a
            clear English sentence.)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>


    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-as-part2">
        <title>Part 2: Proof Choice (Required; Choose One)</title>
        <p>
          Estimated time: 15 minutes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Choose <em>one</em> of the following two proof problems.
          Both problems ask you to write a complete proof; they differ
          in difficulty and which proof strategy you will use.
          Label your submission clearly with <q>Option A</q> or
          <q>Option B.</q>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-as-2a">
        <title>Option A (Standard): A Divisibility Proof by Induction</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Prove by mathematical induction that for all integers
            <m>n \geq 1</m>,
            <md>3 \mid (n^3 + 2n).</md>
            (Here <m>3 \mid m</m> means 3 divides <m>m</m> evenly,
            i.e., <m>m = 3k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.)
          </p>
          <p>
            Your proof must clearly label the base case, state the
            induction hypothesis, and complete the inductive step with
            full justification.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
          <p>
            In the inductive step, write out
            <m>(n+1)^3 + 2(n+1)</m>, expand, and look for a way
            to use both the induction hypothesis and the fact that
            <m>3</m> divides any multiple of <m>3</m>.
          </p>
        </hint>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-as-2b">
        <title>Option B (More Challenging): Irrationality and a Contrapositive</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove by contrapositive: for any integer <m>n</m>, if
                  <m>n^2</m> is divisible by 6, then <m>n</m> is divisible
                  by 6.
                </p>
                <p>
                  (Hint: if <m>6 \nmid n</m>,
                  then <m>n</m> leaves remainder 1, 2, 3, 4, or 5 when
                  divided by 6. Check each case to show
                  <m>6 \nmid n^2</m>. You may use proof by cases within
                  the contrapositive argument.)
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Using part (a), prove by contradiction that <m>\sqrt{6}</m>
                  is irrational. Model your argument closely on the proof
                  that <m>\sqrt{2}</m> is irrational; the key lemma you
                  need is the result from part (a). (Since 6 is not prime,
                  the fact about primes used for <m>\sqrt{5}</m> in
                  <xref ref="ex-mod1-sg-z15-1"/> does not apply directly;
                  part (a) takes its place.)
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>


    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-as-part3">
        <title>Part 3: Reflection</title>
        <p>
          Estimated time: 5 minutes.
        </p>
        <p>
          The following questions are short-answer and are not graded for
          mathematical correctness; they are graded for thoughtfulness
          and specificity. Write 3<ndash/>5 sentences for each. These
          reflections help the instructor understand where you are and
          adapt the course accordingly.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-as-r1">
        <title>Proof Process Reflection</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Describe the process you used to write your proof in
            <xref ref="ex-mod1-as-1b"/>. Where did you get stuck, if at all? How did
            you decide on the value of <m>N</m>? What was the
            relationship between your scratch work and the final proof?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-as-r2">
        <title>Bridge to Bauldry</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In the Bridge Reading (<xref ref="ex-mod1-br-6"/>), you examined Bauldry's proof
            of Theorem 2.3 (Archimedean Order Property). The proof used
            contradiction and the Least Upper Bound Property. Briefly
            explain the logical structure of that proof in your own words:
            what was assumed, what was constructed, and what contradiction
            was reached. (You are not writing a formal proof here; you
            are narrating the argument in plain English.)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-as-r3">
        <title>Looking Ahead</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> proof you wrote in <xref ref="ex-mod1-as-1b"/>
            is the first in a series: <xref ref="ch-mod4"/> will ask you to prove that
            various sequences converge using this same definition. Based
            on your experience with this proof, what do you anticipate
            will be the hardest step in applying the definition to more
            complex sequences? What would you want to practice more?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-as-rubric">
        <title>Assessment Rubric (Informational)</title>
        <p>
          The following rubric describes how your submitted proofs will
          be evaluated. Reading it before you write will help you focus
          your efforts.
        </p>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <col width="14%"/>
          <col width="29%"/>
          <col width="29%"/>
          <col width="28%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell><p>Criterion</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Full credit</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Partial credit</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Minimal credit</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><alert>Logical correctness</alert></p></cell>
            <cell><p>All steps are logically valid; the argument proves exactly the stated claim</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Argument is mostly correct with one fixable gap</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Argument has significant logical errors or proves the wrong statement</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><alert>Quantifier handling</alert></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Quantifiers are written correctly, in the right order, with the right universe</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Minor error in notation but order is correct</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Quantifier order is wrong or domains are missing</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><alert>Proof strategy</alert></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Strategy is named; proof follows the announced structure throughout</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Strategy is correct but not announced, or announced but not fully followed</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Strategy is inappropriate for the claim</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><alert>Required structure</alert></p></cell>
            <cell><p>In <xref ref="ex-mod1-as-1b"/>, the scratch work is labeled, comes before the proof, and finds <m>N</m> with reasons; the formal proof opens <q>Let <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> be given. Choose <m>N = \ldots</m></q> and uses that <m>N</m>. In Option A, the base case, induction hypothesis, and inductive step are labeled</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Scratch work is present but unlabeled or not connected to the <m>N</m> chosen, or the prescribed opening or a required label is missing</p></cell>
            <cell><p>No scratch work, or the proof does not follow the required structure</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><alert>Mathematical prose</alert></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Complete grammatical sentences; no dangling symbols; clear flow</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Mostly prose with some notational shorthand where prose is needed</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Primarily symbolic calculation without connecting sentences</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><alert>Reflection</alert></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Specific, thoughtful, and shows engagement with the mathematical content</p></cell>
            <cell><p>General but relevant observations</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Vague or too brief to be informative</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </paragraphs>


  </section>
